WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.396
C2 is compiling a hot method, settle.

2
00:00:05.396 --> 00:00:08.239
It calls a small helper, requireAmount.

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:12.600
To C2, a call is a wall: it can't see what happens inside.

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:18.598
Say settle always passes 1200, and the helper checks that the amount is in range.

5
00:00:18.598 --> 00:00:20.164
Can C2 skip the check?

6
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.012
Not through the wall.

7
00:00:23.012 --> 00:00:24.963
The check runs on every call.

8
00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:26.998
Inlining removes the wall.

9
00:00:26.998 --> 00:00:32.071
C2 pastes the helper's body into settle, and compiles them as one piece.

10
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:37.739
Now C2 sees 1200 meet the range check, and drops the check at compile time.

11
00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:39.491
That's constant folding.

12
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.988
Saving the call is the small win.

13
00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:46.632
The big one: escape analysis and loop unrolling now see both sides.

14
00:00:46.632 --> 00:00:48.851
That's why inlining comes first.

15
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.777
But each call site gets a budget, in bytes of bytecode.

16
00:00:53.777 --> 00:00:59.654
A site that runs on a quarter or more of the caller's calls is hot: 325 bytes.

17
00:00:59.654 --> 00:01:01.387
Any other site gets 35.

18
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:08.614
This guard checks a refund amount, and builds its error message inline: 49 bytes.

19
00:01:08.614 --> 00:01:10.779
Its site runs on 1 call in 8.

20
00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:12.123
Will C2 inline it?

21
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:13.840
No.

22
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:20.632
49 is over 35, and the budget counts the message bytes, though they almost never run.

23
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:23.429
Move the message into its own method.

24
00:01:23.429 --> 00:01:26.095
The guard drops to 21 bytes, and fits.

25
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:27.910
Here is C2's real log.

26
00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.675
inline (hot) is just how it says yes.

27
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:34.471
Some JDK methods skip the budget.

28
00:01:34.471 --> 00:01:42.573
Long.bitCount is 61 bytes of shifts and masks, but on x86 C2 uses one POPCNT instruction.

29
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.380
These are intrinsics: JDK methods the JIT swaps for hand-written code, so their bytecode size doesn't matter.

30
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.354
In code, the guard calls out for its message.

31
00:01:54.354 --> 00:01:58.979
And Kotlin's countOneBits calls Long.bitCount, the same intrinsic.

32
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:00.580
The rule.

33
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:07.263
Inlining lets C2 see through calls, within 35 bytes, or 325 at a hot site.

34
00:02:07.263 --> 00:02:11.328
Keep cold paths out, and call the JDK method.

35
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.318
Quick check.

36
00:02:12.318 --> 00:02:17.839
A 40-byte method: is it inlined from a site that runs on half the caller's calls?

37
00:02:17.839 --> 00:02:20.224
From one that runs on 1 call in 10?

38
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.226
Yes at the hot site: 40 is under 325.

39
00:02:26.226 --> 00:02:29.432
No at the other: 40 is over 35.
